"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[9095],{409095:n=>{n.exports="# Scripting & Control Flow\n\nSource dialect procedural control (IF/ELSE, WHILE, dynamic SQL, error handling) → DBSQL/PySpark equivalents.\n\n> For DBSQL scripting syntax, load `skills/writing-sql/sql-scripting.md`.\n> For DBSQL dynamic SQL, load `skills/writing-sql/dynamic-sql.md`.\n\n> **Scripting context vs. stored procedure context**: The examples in this file that show T-SQL session-level scripts (outside any stored procedure) use `DECLARE OR REPLACE VARIABLE var TYPE DEFAULT val;` + `SET VAR var = expr;` — the session-variable form. When converting a T-SQL stored procedure, these same patterns must be placed **inside** the procedure's `BEGIN...END` block using `DECLARE var TYPE DEFAULT val;` + `SET var = expr;` (no `OR REPLACE VARIABLE`, no `VAR` keyword). Placing `DECLARE` before the procedure's `AS BEGIN` causes `PARSE_SYNTAX_ERROR`. See `04-procedures-variables.md` for the correct in-procedure structure.\n\n## MSSQL\n\n### Control flow\n\n| T-SQL                          | DBSQL                               | Notes                                      |\n| ------------------------------ | ----------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------ |\n| `IF @x = 1 BEGIN ... END`      | `IF x = 1 THEN ... END IF;`         | No `@` prefix; `BEGIN/END` → `THEN/END IF` |\n| `ELSE IF @x = 2 BEGIN ... END` | `ELSEIF x = 2 THEN ... END IF;`     |                                            |\n| `WHILE @i < 10 BEGIN ... END`  | `WHILE i < 10 DO ... END WHILE;`    |                                            |\n| `BREAK`                        | `LEAVE loop_label;`                 | Label required                             |\n| `CONTINUE`                     | `ITERATE loop_label;`               | Label required                             |\n| `GOTO label`                   | Refactor — no GOTO in DBSQL         | Rewrite as WHILE + conditional LEAVE       |\n| `PRINT 'msg'`                  | `SELECT 'msg';` or log via Notebook | No PRINT statement                         |\n| `RETURN`                       | `RETURN;`                           | Same for procedures                        |\n\n```sql\n-- T-SQL\nIF @count > 0\nBEGIN\n    UPDATE t SET status = 'active';\nEND\nELSE\nBEGIN\n    INSERT INTO t VALUES (1, 'inactive');\nEND;\n\n-- DBSQL\nIF count > 0 THEN\n    UPDATE t SET status = 'active';\nELSE\n    INSERT INTO t VALUES (1, 'inactive');\nEND IF;\n```\n\n### IF EXISTS patterns — two distinct cases\n\n**Case 1 — Row-presence check** (`IF EXISTS(SELECT ... FROM table)`):\n\nChecks whether any row exists in a table or temp table. Do NOT translate to a variable null check.\n\n```sql\n-- T-SQL (row-presence: does #PERIODS have any rows?)\nIF EXISTS(SELECT 1 FROM #PERIODS)\nBEGIN\n    EXEC do_something;\nEND;\n\n-- DBSQL (correct — COUNT into variable, then test)\n-- NOTE: standalone scripting context; inside a procedure use DECLARE + SET (no VAR) inside BEGIN...END\nDECLARE OR REPLACE VARIABLE v_count INT DEFAULT 0;\nSET VAR v_count = (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM temp_periods);\nIF v_count > 0 THEN\n    CALL do_something();\nEND IF;\n```\n\nUse the variable form as the primary translation. Avoid `IF (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM t) > 0` directly in the IF condition — subquery-in-IF validity inside DBSQL procedure bodies is not universally confirmed.\n\n**Case 2 — Object-existence check** (`IF EXISTS` with `OBJECT_ID` or `sys.objects`):\n\nChecks whether a database object (table, procedure, etc.) exists — not whether a table has rows. Translate to the DBSQL DDL form, not a row-count check.\n\n```sql\n-- T-SQL (object-existence: does the procedure sp_abs exist?)\nIF EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM sys.objects WHERE object_id = OBJECT_ID(N'sp_abs') AND type = 'P')\n    DROP PROCEDURE sp_abs;\n\n-- DBSQL\nDROP PROCEDURE IF EXISTS sp_abs;\n\n-- T-SQL (temp table existence)\nIF OBJECT_ID(N'tempdb..#staging', 'U') IS NOT NULL\n    DROP TABLE #staging;\n\n-- DBSQL\nDROP TABLE IF EXISTS staging;\n```\n\nDistinguishing the two patterns: if `sys.objects`, `sys.tables`, `OBJECT_ID()`, or `tempdb..#` appear in the subquery, it is Case 2 (object-existence). If the subquery selects from a user table or temp table without these system references, it is Case 1 (row-presence).\n\n### WHILE loop\n\n```sql\n-- T-SQL\nDECLARE @i INT = 0;\nWHILE @i < 10\nBEGIN\n    INSERT INTO t VALUES (@i);\n    SET @i = @i + 1;\nEND;\n\n-- DBSQL (standalone scripting context; inside a procedure use DECLARE + SET inside BEGIN...END)\nDECLARE OR REPLACE VARIABLE i INT DEFAULT 0;\nWHILE i < 10 DO\n    INSERT INTO t VALUES (i);\n    SET VAR i = i + 1;\nEND WHILE;\n```\n\n### Error handling\n\n```sql\n-- T-SQL\nBEGIN TRY\n    INSERT INTO t VALUES (1);\nEND TRY\nBEGIN CATCH\n    PRINT ERROR_MESSAGE();\nEND CATCH;\n\n-- DBSQL\nBEGIN\n    DECLARE error_msg STRING;\n    DECLARE EXIT HANDLER FOR SQLEXCEPTION\n    BEGIN\n        GET DIAGNOSTICS CONDITION 1 error_msg = MESSAGE_TEXT;\n        SELECT error_msg;\n    END;\n    INSERT INTO t VALUES (1);\nEND;\n```\n\n### RAISERROR / THROW → SIGNAL\n\n```sql\n-- T-SQL\nRAISERROR('Custom error', 16, 1);\nTHROW 50001, 'Custom error', 1;\n\n-- DBSQL\nSIGNAL SQLSTATE '45000' SET MESSAGE_TEXT = 'Custom error';\n```\n\n### Dynamic SQL\n\n```sql\n-- T-SQL\nDECLARE @sql NVARCHAR(MAX) = 'SELECT * FROM ' + @table_name;\nEXEC(@sql);\n-- or: EXEC sp_executesql @sql, N'@param INT', @param = 1;\n\n-- DBSQL (standalone scripting context; inside a procedure use DECLARE inside BEGIN...END)\nDECLARE OR REPLACE VARIABLE sql_stmt STRING DEFAULT CONCAT('SELECT * FROM ', table_name);\nEXECUTE IMMEDIATE sql_stmt;\n-- With runtime parameter binding: ? placeholders, resolved positionally via USING.\n-- Note: sp_executesql uses named parameters (@param); EXECUTE IMMEDIATE uses positional (?).\n-- When migrating multi-parameter calls, the order of arguments in USING must match\n-- the order of ? placeholders in the SQL string.\nDECLARE OR REPLACE VARIABLE sql_with_param STRING DEFAULT CONCAT('SELECT * FROM ', table_name, ' WHERE id = ?');\nEXECUTE IMMEDIATE sql_with_param USING 1;\n```\n\n> For full `EXECUTE IMMEDIATE` and `IDENTIFIER()` syntax, load `writing-sql/dynamic-sql.md`.\n\n### @@ROWCOUNT and system variables\n\nSee `references/tsql/00-quirks.md @@ global variables` (eagerly loaded) for the full mapping.\n\n## SYNAPSE\n\nSame as MSSQL. Note: Synapse T-SQL supports `THROW` but not all `RAISERROR` severity levels — map all to `SIGNAL SQLSTATE '45000'`.\n"}}]);
